From 8df32dbe56d2bd56575f24bd440b2509ae24e5ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ferdinand Vanmaele <info@fvanmaele.de>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 05:03:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [clangd] Index navigation: #include lines, and log why the
 AST is needed

Go-to-definition on an #include line waited for the file's preamble (6.2 s on
a synthetic deal.II project after a restart) although the shard's include graph
already records the headers that exactly this content included.
locateIncludeFromShard reads the spelled name on the cursor's line and answers
with the one direct include whose path ends with it (component boundary). It
does not answer when the content changed since indexing, when no or several
direct includes match, for "." / ".." components, or for macro includes; the
AST path then answers as before. Errors elsewhere in the file do not matter
here, so unlike symbol lookups the HadErrors flag does not block it.

With --log=verbose, navigation that falls back to the AST now says why: file
not open, no stored shard, content changed, indexed with errors, no indexed
reference at the position, or the index did not know the symbol within 5 s.
The shard is loaded once per request.

Synthetic project, second session: #include line 6226 -> 1 ms; other
definitions unchanged (2 - 606 ms). ClangdTests 1412/1412 (new:
IncludeLineMatchesTheAST, IncludeLineNeedsExactlyOneMatchingInclude).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
